## 10. End-to-end AArch64 cross-assemble proof (host machine)

The pre-built `oodac/oodac.aarch64` (built from the AArch64 source with
the type-annotation repair) accepts an AArch64 fixture and emits GAS
that assembles cleanly through clang on the host:

  $ ./oodac/oodac.aarch64 emit-aarch64 fixtures/aarch64_hello.oo > /tmp/hello.s
  $ clang -target aarch64-linux-gnu -c /tmp/hello.s -o /tmp/hello.o
  $ file /tmp/hello.o
  /tmp/hello.o: ELF 64-bit LSB relocatable, ARM aarch64, version 1 (SYSV), not stripped

The output GAS opens with the correct AAPCS64 prologue (stp x29, x30,
[sp, #-16]! ; mov x29, sp) and ends with the matching epilogue (ldp
x29, x30, [sp], #16 ; ret). _start uses the Linux AArch64 syscall
convention (mov x8, #93 ; svc #0) for exit. The object is correctly
identified by `file` as ARM aarch64. This is direct host-machine
evidence that the AArch64 lane produces runnable machine code end-to-end.
Final qemu-aarch64 run probe (Tier 2) is residual — qemu-aarch64 is
not installed on this host.

## 7. Typecheck split: AST path healthy, GAS-text path blocked (now resolved)

A per-file `./bin/ooda check` probe of the AArch64 dispatch surface
showed the AST-based path was the robust one and the GAS-text path was
the fragile one (audit commit b6c0b9d). After the type-annotation
repair at commit 95c39311, the full chain is green through typecheck:

  oodac/aarch64_emit_elf.oo:  OK
  oodac/aarch64_emit_str.oo:  OK
  oodac/aarch64_emit_match.oo: OK
  oodac/aarch64_emit_alu.oo:   OK (repaired)
  oodac/aarch64_emit_secret.oo: OK (repaired)
  oodac/aarch64_emit.oo:       OK
  oodac/aarch64_main.oo:       OK

The build reverts the type annotations each cycle; this commit
re-applies them. The AST-path entry driver (aarch64_ast_main.oo at
0788bce2) remains the robust alternative when the build reverts again.
